1
00:03:44,347 --> 00:03:50,347
A romance of Scotland Yard

2
00:07:28,695 --> 00:07:34,695
Blackmail is a drama, thriller, crime film directed by Alfred Hitchcock.

3
00:11:13,043 --> 00:11:19,043
London, 1929.

4
00:14:57,391 --> 00:15:03,391
Frank Webber, a very busy Scotland Yard detective, seems to be more
interested in his work than in Alice White, his girlfriend.

5
00:18:41,739 --> 00:18:47,739
Feeling herself ignored, Alice agrees to go out with an elegant and
well-mannered artist who invites her to visit his fancy apartment.

6
00:22:26,086 --> 00:22:32,086
Alice White, played by Anny Ondra.

7
00:26:10,434 --> 00:26:16,434
Mrs. White, played by Sara Allgood.

8
00:29:54,782 --> 00:30:00,782
Mr. White, played by Charles Paton.

9
00:33:39,130 --> 00:33:45,130
Detective Frank Webber, played by John Longden.

10
00:37:23,478 --> 00:37:29,478
Tracy, played by Donald Calthrop.

11
00:41:07,826 --> 00:41:13,826
The Artist, played by Cyril Ritchard.

12
00:44:52,173 --> 00:44:58,173
Blackmail is a 1929 British crime thriller film directed by Alfred Hitchcock
and starring Anny Ondra, John Longden, and Cyril Ritchard.

13
00:48:36,521 --> 00:48:42,521
Based on the 1928 play of the same name by Charles Bennett, the film is about a London
woman who is blackmailed after killing a man who tries to rape her.

14
00:52:20,869 --> 00:52:26,869
After starting production as a silent film, British International
Pictures decided to adapt Blackmail into a separate sound film.

15
00:56:05,217 --> 00:56:11,217
It became the first successful European talkie; a silent version was released for cinemas not equipped
for sound (at 6,740 feet), with the sound version (7,136 feet) released at the same time.

16
00:59:49,565 --> 00:59:55,565
Both versions are held in the British Film Institute collection.

17
01:03:33,913 --> 01:03:39,913
Blackmail is frequently cited as the first British sound feature film.

18
01:07:18,260 --> 01:07:24,260
It was voted the best British film of 1929 in a
UK poll the year it was released.

19
01:11:02,608 --> 01:11:08,608
In 2017 a poll of 150 actors, directors, writers, producers and critics for Time
Out magazine ranked Blackmail as the 59th best British film ever.

20
01:14:46,956 --> 01:14:52,956
On 1 January 2025, the film's copyright expired in the
United States, and is now in the public domain.

21
01:18:31,304 --> 01:18:37,304
The film explores themes of london, england, blackmail, scotland
yard, based on play or musical, black and white.

22
01:22:15,652 --> 01:22:21,652
The tone is emotional and intense.
